artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a mathematical framework for adaptive cancer therapy in which a heterogeneous tumor population evolves under selective pressure imposed by treatment, with subpopulations differing in their sensitivity and resistance profiles to a panel of available drugs D = {d_1, ..., d_n}. A treatment schedule is a measurable function s : [0, T] → 2^D specifying which subset of drugs is administered at each time t, possibly subject to constraints on toxicity, total dose, switching frequency, and minimum/maximum dose intervals. Tumor dynamics are governed by a model (e.g., a Lotka–Volterra system, agent-based model, or stochastic birth-death process) that captures competition between sensitive and resistant clones, with treatment-dependent fitness landscapes that may include collateral sensitivity (resistance to one drug inducing sensitivity to another).

Formulate and justify a set of design principles ("best practices") for adaptive therapy algorithms in the multi-drug setting that address the following:

(a) Methods to reduce the combinatorial complexity of choosing drug subsets, dosing schedules, sequencing, and switching times, so that the search space of schedules s(·) can be optimized tractably.

(b) Criteria specifying when each of the following strategies is preferable, with explicit mathematical conditions on the tumor model (fitness landscape, cross-resistance structure, growth rates, competition coefficients) under which each is optimal or near-optimal:
   (i) evolutionary cycling between drugs;
   (ii) double-bind therapy, where a first treatment is chosen so that the evolutionary response creates vulnerability to a second;
   (iii) primary–secondary adaptive therapy, alternating between a primary and secondary agent based on tumor burden thresholds;
   (iv) extinction therapy, applying a first strike to reduce tumor size followed by a second strike to drive remaining cells to extinction.

(c) Identification of the clinical and preclinical evidence (biomarkers, monitoring frequency, response endpoints) required to validate such multi-drug adaptive algorithms.

Provide a rigorous answer or a constructive framework that resolves (a)–(c).

